  "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
